ltsp (6.3.32-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sound for RDP sessions: PipeWire runs for root on the client. xfreerdp's
    /sound:sys:pulse and /microphone:sys:pulse need a PulseAudio-compatible
    server, and without one FreeRDP ends the whole connection right after
    it is made. The images built since the move to systemd had none - LTSP
    5 started "pulseaudio --system" from an init script this package does
    not ship - so the RDP options a fleet brought from LTSP 5 failed, and
    only worked once the sound options were taken out.
  * The new init-ltsp.d/50-pipewire lets root linger when SOUND_DAEMON is
    pipewire, so logind starts root's user manager at boot and with it
    PipeWire, WirePlumber and the pulse socket in /run/user/0. Debian's
    pipewire and pipewire-pulse units carry ConditionUser=!root, so the
    script also clears that condition with drop-ins in /etc/systemd/user;
    without them user@0 came up and skipped PipeWire silently. The new
    screen-session.d/S20-pulse-runtime gives every screen XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
    pointing there; without it libpulse looked nowhere. Tested on an Intel
    NUC N4505 with ldm and /sound:sys:pulse /microphone:sys:pulse: sound
    over HDMI, chosen by WirePlumber, and the microphone path to the server.
  * ltsp_config.d/25sound sets SOUND_DAEMON=pipewire when pipewire-pulse is
    in the image. It looked only for /usr/bin/pulseaudio, so with PipeWire
    SOUND stayed unset.
  * ltsp-client-core recommends pipewire-pulse and wireplumber, so
    ltsp-build-client brings them in, and suggests pulseaudio-utils and
    alsa-utils for diagnosis. lts.conf(5) explains the chain.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:48:03 +0200

ltsp (6.3.31-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A Debian build writes the PXE menu. Nothing did: the Ubuntu plugins run
    the chroot's kernel hook, Debian had no equivalent, and
    cleanup.d/50-update-kernels only runs for ltsp-update-image --cleanup.
    A build left the TFTP tree with whatever menu was there before - fine
    on a server that had one, no pxelinux.cfg at all on a new server, with
    clients stopping at the boot: prompt. It also meant that none of
    update-kernels' own fixes, those of 6.3.28 included, ever reached a
    client through a build. The new Debian/035-pxe-menu runs update-kernels
    in the chroot before 035-update-kernels copies /boot into the TFTP tree.
  * Boot parameters come from the server, kernel recognition from the image.
    The server's /etc/ltsp/update-kernels.conf, where an administrator puts
    something like ipv6.disable=1, is laid over the chroot's for the run,
    without its KERNEL_NAMES, INITRD_NAME, KERNEL_PREFIX, KERNEL_SUFFIX and
    LIST_KERNELS_* - a server's copy is easily years old, and an old
    KERNEL_NAMES recognises no trixie kernel. The chroot's file, a conffile,
    is restored afterwards.
  * NBD_ROOT_NAME keeps its leading slash. The export is named after the
    image's path, /srv/ltsp/amd64 or /opt/ltsp/amd64, and the initramfs
    connects with exactly that; stripping the slash named an export that
    does not exist. ldm's update check, the only user, then compared the
    running image against nothing, and with LDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE=True would
    have announced a reboot at every login screen. Bare names written the
    LTSP 5 way, nbdroot=server:ltsp_amd64, are unaffected.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:00:37 +0200

ltsp (6.3.30-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Printers attached to a client are shared again. PRINTER_<n>_DEVICE in
    lts.conf now gets an ltsp-printer@<n> instance at every boot, from the
    new init-ltsp.d/50-printers, which runs jetpipe for it. LTSP 5 started
    the printers from an init script this package stopped shipping when it
    moved to systemd, and nothing took its place: since then no client has
    shared a printer, and nothing said so - the print server simply could
    not connect. Found on a production lts.conf with three USB printers.
  * jetpipe is rewritten for printer devices only, and no longer needs
    pyserial. "import serial" at the top made it fail at start for USB
    printers too, and a thin client image does not carry python3-serial.
    The serial side had not worked under Python 3 anyway: its options were
    looked up in the wrong scope and ignored, and the port was opened twice,
    which ended the daemon after the first job.
  * jetpipe never creates the device. The old "open(device, 'wb')" turned a
    job for an unplugged printer into a regular file under /dev, in RAM -
    the job vanished without an error, and the kernel could no longer create
    the device when the printer was plugged in. A job sent then is refused,
    and the print server retries.
  * jetpipe stops a job on a write or connection error instead of retrying
    it in a loop at full load, abandons a job that sends nothing for ten
    minutes, binds with SO_REUSEADDR so a restart does not fail with
    "Address already in use", and logs each job to the journal.
  * ltsp-client-core recommends python3, which jetpipe is written in.
  * lts.conf(5) documents PRINTER_<n>_DEVICE and PRINTER_<n>_PORT, which it
    did not mention at all, and jetpipe(8) is rewritten.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:13:23 +0200

ltsp (6.3.29-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Exactly the screens lts.conf names are started, on the terminals it
    names, as under LTSP 5 - a greeter on 05 alone, on 06 and 07, on five
    terminals; 07 is no different from any other. The new
    init-ltsp.d/50-screens enables one ltsp-screen@ instance per SCREEN_nn
    at boot and masks ldm.service. Until now 07 came from ldm.service, which
    ran whether lts.conf named 07 or not, and every other screen had to be
    enabled by hand inside the image: an lts.conf carried over from LTSP 5
    with SCREEN_06=ldm next to SCREEN_07=ldm booted to one greeter, and
    Ctrl+Alt+F6 showed an empty terminal with nothing failed and nothing
    logged. Found on a production lts.conf that gives the two screens their
    own RDP servers and options. Decided on every boot from lts.conf alone,
    so adding or removing a screen needs no new image.
  * ltsp-screen@.service carries what ldm.service did for the greeter, now
    on whichever terminal it runs: ordering after plymouth and logind,
    SIGPIPE at its default, KillMode=mixed, a user-session utmp entry, and
    the terminal deallocated when the screen stops.
  * Only the default screen skips the wait for its terminal. 6.3.1 filled
    in SCREEN_nn_WAIT_FGCONSOLE=False for every ldm screen, which was
    harmless while only one was ever started; with two, both X servers
    start at once, each brings its own terminal to the front, and whichever
    comes up last is the one on the monitor. SCREEN_DEFAULT - or the
    highest-numbered screen - now starts in front and the others wait to be
    switched to, which is how LTSP 5 behaved. A value set in lts.conf is
    left alone, so multiseat setups that name it keep working unchanged.
  * ltsp-screen@.service can start screens 10 to 12. It handed screen_session
    a fixed "0%i", which made tty10 into "010", and screen_session accepts
    only two digits. The padding is printf's now.
  * SCREEN_DEFAULT=08 and 09 are accepted. printf read the leading zero as
    octal and refused both.
  * A mistake in lts.conf no longer ends the boot in a kernel panic. One
    forgotten closing quote was enough: flex has no rule for a lone quote,
    so its default rule copied it to stdout, ltsp_config.d/01-getltscfg
    evaluated getltscfg's output with eval - and a syntax error inside eval
    ends a non-interactive shell outright; the "|| true" behind it never
    runs. That shell is init-ltsp, running as PID 1. Reported from a
    client, after a missing quote. Fixed in two places, so that either
    alone would have prevented it:
  * 01-getltscfg reads getltscfg's output one assignment at a time, checks
    each with sh -n and leaves out the ones that do not parse, naming them
    in the kernel log - by name only, a value can be a password. Only
    checked lines go into /run/ltsp/ltsp_config_env, which other scripts
    source without a check of their own. getltscfg's own messages, which
    went to /dev/null, go to the log as well. ltsp-shadow's fallback for a
    missing cache does the same.
  * getltscfg accepts what an administrator writes without thinking: single
    quotes, a missing closing quote (the value then runs to the end of the
    line, with a warning naming the line), Windows line endings, and a
    comment on the last line without a newline. Each of the last three used
    to make it drop every setting from that point on, with exit status 0
    and the message thrown away - a whole lts.conf saved on Windows came
    out empty, and the client booted as if unconfigured. %option nodefault
    keeps flex from copying any character to stdout again.
  * getltscfg exits with status 3 after a syntax error. The parser still
    stops there, and the settings after it are still missing, but it is
    now said, with the line.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:37:46 +0200

ltsp (6.3.28-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The PXE menu names the kernel by its symlink. The main entries now
    point at vmlinuz and initrd.img - which the kernel package maintains in
    /boot and ltsp-update-kernels copies into the TFTP tree - instead of at
    the version they found when the menu was written. A new kernel then
    needs no new menu. A pinned version did: the menu kept naming a kernel
    that cleanup_kernels removed once its modules left the image, and a
    client got a TFTP error, or booted a kernel the image has no modules
    for. Where /boot has no such symlinks the version is still written, as
    before.
  * Kernels are recognised by their current Debian names. The filter
    expected vmlinuz-<version>-<abi>-<flavour>, the form Debian used until
    it moved the ABI into the version: 7.2.6+deb13-amd64 and
    6.12.9+bpo-amd64 have one dash where the filter wanted two, so no
    trixie kernel was recognised at all. The per-version submenu stayed
    empty, the vmlinuz-<flavour> links were never renewed and pointed at
    deleted files, and 50-vmlinuz could not create the /boot links it
    exists to create. The last dash now separates the flavour, which also
    covers the old form and Proxmox kernels.
  * ltsp-client-core recommends pxelinux and syslinux-common. Without
    them in the chroot update-kernels writes no menu at all and says so in
    one line nobody reads during a build, and the TFTP tree keeps whatever
    menu an older image left behind - with the kernel names of that older
    image. Found on a production clone, where a trixie image went out with
    a menu from June 2025.

