ltsp (6.3.25-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-support reads its list of clients from more than one place, and
    from other machines: SUPPORT_LEASES takes several sources, each either
    a path or [user@]host:/path read over ssh. The list comes from
    whoever answers DHCP, so the moment a site puts a load balancer in
    front of several session servers and support is run somewhere other
    than the boot server, the file is not on this machine at all and the
    window was empty. Two sources also cover a pair of boot servers
    failing over; a client in both is listed once, by address.
  * A source that cannot be read is named with ssh's own words while the
    others are still shown, and the reading happens off the main loop - an
    ssh connection that has to time out would otherwise freeze the window
    for a quarter of a minute with no way to type a client name.
  * The same field now takes a user name. It asks the clients that have
    booted, in parallel, which of them has that user signed in, and offers
    it - nothing is stored, and the answer is gone when the session ends.
    A name is treated as a machine when the client list or the resolver
    knows it, and as a person otherwise.
  * lts.conf(5), section KERBEROS, says three things it left to be worked
    out: that an address beats a hostname there, because naming the
    controllers is what takes DNS out of the path and a name puts it back;
    that TIMESERVER belongs beside those settings, since a clock more than
    five minutes out is refused and reads to the user like a wrong
    password; and how to check what a client made of it - /etc/krb5.conf on
    the running client, and what the log says when the realm never arrived.
  * It asks the clients rather than the session servers deliberately.
    Behind a load balancer every session arrives from the balancer's
    address, so a session server knows which balancer somebody came
    through, which does not find a desk. The client knows: ldm starts the
    session there as xfreerdp3 ... /u:<user>, and that command line is in
    its own process list. A domain spelling of the name, DOMAIN\\user or
    user@domain, matches on the bare name.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 22:06:15 +0200

ltsp (6.3.24-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The xorg.conf the client assembles is now one X.Org will use. It wrote
    a Device section and nothing else: no Screen referring to it, no
    ServerLayout at all. X.Org does not take that as a hint - it keeps its
    own automatic layout, drops the configured screen with "Screen 0
    deleted because of no matching config section", and carries on with
    whatever driver its probe liked. So XSERVER appeared to do nothing.
  * Where it did more than nothing it was worse. On a client whose kernel
    provides a DRM device and whose image carries the whole
    xserver-xorg-video-all set, the automatic probe reached fbdev and vesa
    as well and the server died with "Cannot run in framebuffer mode.
    Please specify busIDs for all framebuffer devices" - every time, so
    screen_session restarted it for ever and the client showed a text
    console instead of a greeter. Measured on a Hyper-V client with
    hyperv_drm; the same shape of failure is reachable on any machine
    where more than one driver claims the card.
  * 09-krb5-conf writes a kpasswd_server as well, defaulting to the first
    controller. A KDC answers sign-ins on port 88 and the password service
    answers changes on 464, and they are separate entries; with controllers
    named in lts.conf the DNS lookup is off, so nothing else could find that
    service. Signing in worked and changing an expired password failed with
    "Cannot find KDC for realm" - found at a real greeter against a real
    Samba AD, after the same code had worked from a machine whose krb5.conf
    happened to name one. KRB5_KPASSWD_SERVER overrides it.
  * A ServerFlags section with AutoAddGPU and AutoBindGPU off goes with it,
    written only when a driver was asked for. Without it the automatically
    added GPU screen wins over the configured one even with Screen and
    ServerLayout present - tried on the client: Screen and ServerLayout
    alone still failed, a BusID did not help, no configuration at all did
    not either, and switching the automatic addition off worked on the
    first try.

