ltsp (6.3.23-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The client stops deleting unit files to turn services off. It now
    disables them, and masks what disabling cannot reach. Deleting was
    the wrong operation from the start: a unit file carries more than the
    service, and removing one takes its aliases with it - which is how
    systemd-logind once vanished together with its D-Bus name, leaving
    Xorg unable to get a session and screen_session respawning it several
    times a second. systemd's own answer is disable and mask, both
    reversible, both visible in systemctl is-enabled.
  * It happens before systemd starts - init-ltsp ends with exec
    /sbin/init - so systemctl is used offline: --root=/ --no-reload.
    Measured on trixie, and the measurements shaped the code: disable
    removes enablement symlinks under /etc but leaves the ones a package
    ships in /usr/lib, so those are masked instead and named in the log;
    mask covers only the unit named, so cups.socket is masked alongside
    cups.service; disabling a unit that does not exist prints six lines of
    explanation and returns 0, so the list is filtered first.
  * SysV services are disabled in a call of their own. systemctl hands a
    non-native name to systemd-sysv-install, which chroots and runs
    update-rc.d; when that fails it takes the whole call with it, and one
    SysV name in the batch left every native unit enabled. The old hook's
    own removal of /etc/rc?.d entries is gone: update-rc.d is the
    supported way and it turns S01 into K01 properly.
  * DISABLE_SYSTEM_SERVICES and MASK_SYSTEM_SERVICES join the older RM_
    spellings, which keep working - a site's lts.conf is not rewritten
    because a hook was. cups, avahi-daemon and bluetooth are masked on
    thin clients by default, because all three start on demand and
    disabling them achieves nothing.
  * DISABLE_GETTYS finally does something. It named tty2 to tty6 on the
    removal list, and there is no tty2.service on a systemd system -
    gettys come from the getty@.service template, started on demand by
    logind up to NAutoVTs. The five entries matched no file, so every
    client kept its text logins while the configuration said otherwise.
    Now it writes NAutoVTs=1 and ReserveVT=0 into a logind drop-in. On a
    thin client a console is a root prompt, so this is not cosmetic.
  * lts.conf(5) documents RDP_PREAUTH_CHANGE_PASSWORD, which ldm 3.4.8-1
    adds: an expired domain password can be changed at the greeter instead
    of only being reported. A KDC still issues a ticket for kadmin/changepw
    when the password has expired, and that is what makes it possible from
    a thin client at all. The section also says what a KDC that does not
    answer does - it stops the login, and says the password is not the
    problem.
  * 50-disable-inittab-entries is gone with it: it edited /etc/inittab,
    which Debian has not had since sysvinit, and checked for the file
    first - so it did nothing at all, every boot, for years.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:42:28 +0200

ltsp (6.3.22-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-shadow lets support watch a client's screen and use its
    keyboard and mouse, with the user's consent. It runs over the ssh
    access added in 6.3.21-1: the shadow server binds to 127.0.0.1 and
    nothing else, so it is reachable only through the ssh connection
    that started it, the authentication is a key from lts.conf rather
    than a second password, access is withdrawn by editing one file, and
    the server dies with the ssh session.
  * The user is asked first, in their own session, and a marker stays on
    screen for as long as anyone is watching. REMOTE_SUPPORT_CONSENT
    turns off the question for an unattended machine; nothing turns off
    the marker. Watching someone's screen at work is not a technical
    question, and a tool that can watch silently is the wrong tool.
  * ltsp-support is the server side of it, and a desktop menu entry, so
    starting a support session is not a line of ssh with a port forward
    typed correctly under pressure. It lists the clients that have
    booted, asks the one chosen, waits for the user to agree, and opens
    the picture. SUPPORT_KEY, SUPPORT_PORT, SUPPORT_RDP_CLIENT and
    SUPPORT_LEASES in /etc/ltsp/ltsp-server.conf where a site differs.
    It is a GTK3 program too, and says what is missing rather than
    failing quietly.
  * REMOTE_SUPPORT_MONITOR picks one screen of a multi-monitor client,
    so a support session need not carry 5120x1440.
  * REMOTE_SUPPORT_DISPLAY, or an argument, picks the X display where a
    client runs more than one. Without it the screen on the active
    virtual terminal is taken, which is the one the user is looking at.
  * The two windows the user sees are ltsp-shadow-ui, a GTK3 program of
    its own rather than a dialog tool: the greeter on that client is
    GTK3 already, so they match what the user has been looking at, and
    it buys a marker that never takes the keyboard, a countdown that
    says how long is left, and a refusal on Escape and on the window
    being closed. Needs python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0 and
    freerdp3-shadow-x11 in the image; suggested, not depended on, so an
    image that does not want remote support pays nothing.
  * 09-krb5-conf writes /etc/krb5.conf on the client from lts.conf:
    KRB5_REALM, KRB5_KDC_n, KRB5_DNS_LOOKUP, KRB5_ADMIN_SERVER. Without
    it RDP_PREAUTH=True finds no realm on a thin client, skips itself
    and says so in the log - which from the outside looks exactly like a
    check that is working. Naming the controllers also takes DNS out of
    the failover path: in an Active Directory the SRV records live on
    the controllers, so a client whose only nameserver is the one that
    just died cannot look up the one that is still running. Order
    matters - a controller that drops packets costs about 18 seconds
    before the next is asked, measured, and kdc_timeout does not shorten
    it.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:03:09 +0200

ltsp (6.3.21-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two new init-ltsp.d hooks make ssh on a client something a site can
    actually use, rather than something that is installed and then
    deleted at boot.
  * 07-ssh-host-keys generates fresh host keys per client. The image
    carries one set, so every client presented the same fingerprint and
    the private halves reached all of them over NBD. SSH_HOST_KEYS=image
    keeps the old behaviour. Runs only when sshd is installed and
    KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES names ssh.
  * 08-ssh-authorized-keys authorises keys for root from lts.conf, as
    SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEY_0 and so on. Without it a client with
    openssh-server still cannot be logged into: sshd_config is at
    Debian's PermitRootLogin prohibit-password and the image has no other
    account. Root login stays key-only.
  * lts.conf.5 documents both, and says plainly what they are worth: both
    are read from a file fetched over unauthenticated TFTP, which is the
    exposure the boot chain already has and no more.

