ltsp (6.3.19-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5): a new section, LOCK KEYS OVER RDP, for the Caps Lock and
    Num Lock state showing one thing on the keyboard and doing another in
    the session. Read in xrdp's own source rather than guessed at:

    - xrdp does implement the client's Synchronize event. xrdp_wm_key_sync()
      is commented "happens when client gets focus and sends key modifier
      info", resets all three lock states and applies what arrived;
      afterwards xrdp tracks them by watching for scancodes 58, 69 and 70.

    - Which means the synchronisation happens at focus, and ldm starts
      xfreerdp full screen: focus is taken once and never lost, so there is
      no second synchronisation for the rest of the session. Whatever is
      wrong at the start stays wrong.

    - And there is no way back. The protocol's Set Keyboard Indicators PDU,
      with which a server updates a client's LEDs, is not implemented -
      "indicator" appears in xrdp's sources only in ISO length fields. Still
      true in the current release, 0.10.6.1; everything its notes say about
      keyboards between 0.10.1 and 0.10.6.1 concerns layouts.

    So the starting state is what can be fixed, and X_NUMLOCK is how: it
    sets the client's state before the session begins and the focus
    synchronisation carries it across. That is the intended mechanism, not a
    workaround. If they still drift, RDP_OPTIONS="/kbd:unicode:on" sends the
    character instead of the scancode - xrdp accepts those, RDP_INPUT_UNICODE
    reaches xrdp_wm_key_unicode() - at the price of applying to character
    keys only, and needing FreeRDP 3, which is what Debian 13 has. FreeRDP 2
    has no such option and is no longer in the distribution at all.

  * numlockx joins the recommended package list in README, README.de and
    QuickInstall. X_NUMLOCK has always said it needs numlockx in the image,
    while the list this project recommends left it out - and without it the
    directive does nothing, silently.

  * The README package tables say what is current again, with a line saying
    they will not stay that way: the archive's own Packages file and the
    releases page are the authority.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:16:30 +0200

ltsp (6.3.18-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The client no longer spends ten seconds of every shutdown on a name
    lookup nobody asked for.

        A stop job is running for Network Time Synchronization (10s / 1min 30s)

    That is not a timeout expiring - the unit has none of its own, so the
    limit is 90 seconds. It is systemd-timesyncd resolving an NTP server
    name in a worker thread that SIGTERM cannot interrupt, and the glibc
    resolver giving up after five seconds, twice. Three changes, each
    closing one way into that:

    - LTSP now empties FallbackNTP alongside NTP. Debian's fallback is
      0.debian.pool.ntp.org and its siblings; a time server that stops
      answering used to land the client on those names, and a client
      segment resolves neither them nor anything else.

    - The NTP server DHCP offers is used when lts.conf names none. Option
      42 was already captured by the initramfs as NTPSVR and then ignored,
      so a site handing out a time server with its leases had to name it a
      second time in lts.conf.

    - With no source from either, a thin client masks systemd-timesyncd and
      says so on the console. What would be left is that internet pool, and
      a thin client belongs on a segment with no route off it: the daemon
      buys nothing there and costs those ten seconds every time. A fat
      client is left alone - it runs its session locally and is an ordinary
      machine on an ordinary network.

    TIMESERVER=none forces it off, an address avoids the lookup altogether,
    and naming the pool asks for the pool deliberately.

  * 50-set-time also stops writing /etc/ntp.conf when no ntpd is installed,
    and stops running sed over /etc/default/ntpdate when that file is not
    there. Neither achieved anything in an image without those packages,
    which the standard one is.

  * lts.conf(5): TIMESERVER described as what it now is. The old text said
    it needed ntpdate and was unnecessary where systemd already
    synchronises, which had it backwards.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 14:27:02 +0200

ltsp (6.3.17-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5): #ldm-preferences, the fifth name a theme's greeter.css can
    address, new in ldm 3.4.5-1. It is a button rather than a label and is
    in the list for the same reason the labels are: it is drawn with relief
    NONE, so it has no ground of its own and sits straight on the
    background image. A theme that lightens the labels for a dark
    photograph and cannot reach this one leaves a single word in the
    default near-black.

