ltsp (6.3.12-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5): a new section, THE GREETER'S APPEARANCE, for what the
    login screen says and how it looks - the two being separate things.

    The wording comes from LDM_USERNAME_PROMPT, LDM_PASSWORD_PROMPT and
    LDM_VERIFY_MESSAGE, new in ldm 3.4.2-1. Each is Pango markup passed to
    the greeter unchanged, as LDM_CLOCK_FORMAT already was, so a value can
    carry emphasis or a colour of its own - which is the shortest way to
    make one line readable on a dark background without writing a theme.
    The defaults are the previous strings and still translated, so nothing
    has to be set to keep a localised image localised.

    Everything visual comes from the theme's greeter.css, and two things
    about it were nowhere written down. First, LDM_THEME looks files up one
    at a time and falls back to the ltsp theme per file - which is why a
    theme can be two images and nothing else, and equally why a theme that
    does carry a greeter.css replaces the default one outright instead of
    adding to it. Second, the greeter now names four of its labels
    (#ldm-prompt, #ldm-status, #ldm-host, #ldm-clock), so they can be
    styled separately; before, the only selector that reached them was
    "label", which also catches the text inside every button.

