ltsp (6.3.11-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-info: name both places a client reads lts.conf from, for every
    architecture found, and say which of the two wins:

        amd64:
          present  /var/lib/tftpboot/ltsp/amd64/lts.conf   served by tftp - takes precedence
          missing  /opt/ltsp/amd64/etc/lts.conf            in the chroot - the fallback

    The order is not a guess: init-ltsp.d/05-getltsconffile fetches the file
    over tftp from the directory the kernel was loaded from and writes it
    over /etc/lts.conf in the running client, but only when the transfer
    returns a non-empty file. So the tftp copy wins whenever it is there
    and the one in the image is what is left when it is not - including
    when the client asks for a directory the server does not have, which
    fails silently and is the usual reason an lts.conf that looks right has
    no effect. -v prints the contents of each under its path, so the two
    can be compared where both exist.

    When no architecture can be found at all, the paths are printed anyway
    with <arch> in place of the name: a path to create is more use than
    silence.

    A directory under the TFTP root counts as an architecture only when it
    carries a kernel or an lts.conf. Anything can end up in such a tree - a
    stray mkdir, a command's help text pasted at a shell - and listing each
    one buries the real ones. Those directories are still reported, with no
    boot file under them, so nothing is hidden by the filter.

  * ltsp-build-client(8) and the lab HOWTO: name the firmware package you
    want, never the firmware-linux metapackage. It reaches
    firmware-amd-graphics, 93 MB of firmware for AMD graphics chips, onto a
    client that may have none; firmware-intel-graphics is 32 MB. The
    documented example said firmware-linux, which was wrong advice on an
    image every client pulls over the network at every boot.

  * Same two documents: a backports kernel is not just newer. Debian split
    the kernel package after 6.12 - in trixie
    linux-image-6.12.107+deb13-amd64 is one 106 MB package holding kernel
    and modules, in backports the same kernel is linux-base plus
    linux-binary plus a 153 MB linux-modules, 62 MB more and a package name
    in the build log that was never there before. And it depends on
    "dracut | initramfs-tools | linux-initramfs-tool" where the trixie
    kernel names initramfs-tools first. Backports packages are installed
    before --late-packages, so ltsp-client-core has not pulled
    initramfs-tools in yet and apt takes dracut - whose initramfs carries
    none of LTSP's hooks. Ask for it by name in --early-packages.

  * ltsp-build-client(8), HOWTO: the components on the backports line come
    from --components, so a package outside main needs its component named
    there as well.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:47:14 +0200

ltsp (6.3.10-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-build-client: --backports-mirror on its own no longer forces
    packages out of the backports suite. It defaulted to ltsp-client,
    ltsp-client-core, ltspfsd, ltspfsd-core and ldm - a list from the days
    when Debian shipped LTSP 5, and not one of those packages has been in
    Debian for years. Each was handed to apt as package/suite, so the plain
    option could not do anything but fail, with

        E: Release 'trixie-backports' for 'ldm' was not found

    which reads as if the suite were unknown. It is not: trixie-backports
    exists, answers, and carries 3279 packages in main/amd64 - just none of
    those five. Only what --backports-packages names is taken from
    backports now, and the build says so when a mirror is given without
    any.

  * ltsp-info: report the TFTP directories in their own right instead of
    once per chroot. The client reads its lts.conf and its kernel from
    there and from nowhere else, so that listing matters whether or not a
    matching chroot exists - and it often does not, because the chroot was
    built under --base elsewhere or removed once its image was in place. A
    server that boots clients perfectly well reported no lts.conf at all.
    Which directories are looked at now comes from TFTP_DIRS and
    TFTP_BOOT_DIR, as everywhere else in LTSP; ltsp-info was the one place
    that hardcoded the paths and so ignored ltsp-server.conf.

  * ltsp-info: new --base/-b option, so the chroot listing can be pointed
    at a build tree kept apart from the running one. When no chroot is
    found, say so and name the directory that was searched, rather than
    printing nothing.

  * ltsp-info(1), ltsp-build-client(8) and the lab HOWTO document all of
    the above, including how to check a backports suite (Packages.xz, not
    Packages.gz - Debian no longer ships the gzipped index, and the 404
    that comes back is 300 bytes of HTML that a careless check reads as an
    empty suite).

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:08:42 +0200

